From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 22:23:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: core: hold the late cleanup off during a
 system suspend

The unused-regulator cleanup runs from delayed work 30 s after late
init, on the system workqueue, which is not frozen for a suspend. A
suspend entered inside that window runs it with devices suspended: on
the Gemini a suspend-to-idle begun 28 s after boot had the cleanup read
the bq25890 VBUS regulator through a suspended I2C adapter, and
__i2c_transfer() warned.

Queue it on system_freezable_wq instead, so a suspend holds it until
the system resumes. Nothing else changes: it still runs once, at least
30 s after late init.
---
 drivers/regulator/core.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/core.c b/drivers/regulator/core.c
index dc5d67767..26f2fe731 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/core.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/core.c
@@ -6881,9 +6881,13 @@ static int __init regulator_init_complete(void)
 	 * a bit and hope we waited long enough.  It'd be better if
 	 * we'd only do this on systems that need it, and a kernel
 	 * command line option might be useful.
+	 *
+	 * The cleanup reads and switches regulators, possibly over buses
+	 * that are suspended with the system, so it must not run during a
+	 * suspend: use a freezable workqueue, which holds it until resume.
 	 */
-	schedule_delayed_work(&regulator_init_complete_work,
-			      msecs_to_jiffies(30000));
+	queue_delayed_work(system_freezable_wq, &regulator_init_complete_work,
+			   msecs_to_jiffies(30000));
 
 	return 0;
 }
-- 
